= In Committee: the Trade Union Bill

Backbenchers want to go further and make union members opt in to paying the political levy, instead of having to opt out. It would cut off much of the Labour Party's money. The TUC has offered a deal: if the government drops the idea, unions will tell every member of the right to opt out.

- @amend_yes_trade_union: Make members opt in to the levy.
- @amend_no_trade_union: Take the TUC's deal.

@amend_yes_trade_union
subtitle: Labour's finances suffer. The wets call it partisan.
on-arrival: dry_dissent -= 3; right_dissent -= 2; wet_dissent += 4; bill_trade_union_tough = 1; bill_trade_union = 3; bill_trade_union_amend = 2; bill_trade_union_due = time + 1

The amendment is carried. Labour says the Conservatives are trying to bankrupt the opposition; the party treasurer at Central Office says nothing at all.

@amend_no_trade_union
subtitle: The bill passes more smoothly. The dries are disappointed.
on-arrival: dry_dissent += 2; wet_dissent -= 2; union_power -= 1; bill_trade_union = 3; bill_trade_union_amend = 2; bill_trade_union_due = time + 1

The deal is done at the Department of Employment. The unions will write to their members about opting out, and the government will leave the levy alone.

@lords_trade_union
title: The Lords Amend the Trade Union Bill
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and bill_trade_union = 4 and bill_trade_union_lords = 1
new-page: true
face-image: img/events/westminster.jpg

= The Lords Amend the Trade Union Bill

Peers led by former Labour ministers vote to let unions hold strike ballots at the workplace, by a show of hands in a secret booth if need be, rather than by post.

- @lords_yes_trade_union: Accept the Lords amendment.
- @lords_no_trade_union: Overturn it in the Commons.

@lords_yes_trade_union
subtitle: Workplace ballots are easier to win for the militants.
on-arrival: wet_dissent -= 1; dry_dissent += 2; bill_trade_union_watered = 1; bill_trade_union_lords = 2; bill_trade_union_due = time + 1

The government accepts workplace ballots as an alternative to the post. Royal Assent should follow within the month.

